 Table~\ref{tab:abort} reports abort performance across environments and rules. At the same $\delta{=}0.10$, Sokoban and SWE-bench save only 7.1\% and 3.1\% of doomed token spend under the classifier-only rule, with false-abort rates of 5.8\% and 0\%, respectively. Combining the interval trigger makes the policy more conservative at fixed $\delta$ (Sokoban savings fall to 1.9\%; SWE-bench to 0\%) and does not recover Warehouse-scale savings. The 86.3\% figure is therefore a Warehouse result for the feasibility gate alone, not a general Budget Guard savings rate, and it does not use conformal remaining-budget intervals. Two further cautions: the dollars are simulated Warehouse dollars, not comparable to BAGEN's 28--64\% token savings; and the observed 0\% false-abort rate should be read as consistent with the $\delta{=}0.10$ bound on a small test set, not as proof that the true rate is zero.
 
+\paragraph{A worked example.}
+Table~\ref{tab:case_study} follows the first held-out Sokoban rollout in the seed-0 test stream turn by turn; the rollout is fixed by that rule, not chosen by hand. The agent's self-reported intervals cover only the first prefix: from turn~2 they sit entirely above the truth, and the last one reserves 400 to 900 tokens for a run that needed 267. The guard's split-CQR intervals are wider but contain the truth at every turn, spend so far plus their upper bound stays under the budget throughout, and the $\delta{=}0.10$ gate of \S\ref{sec:econ} scores the run 0.95 to 0.99 against an abort threshold of 0.02, so a deployer could have certified it from turn~1; it succeeded inside budget. The example is representative: over all 30 test rollouts of this split, split-CQR covers more prefixes than the self-report on 22, ties on 6 and trails on 2 (windowed-CQR: 24, 5 and 1). Figure~\ref{fig:case_study} (Appendix~\ref{app:breadth}) plots the rollout.
