+Our breadth figures are based on a single-split: on seed~0, windowed-CQR reaches at least 0.85 coverage in 18 of 19 runs. Search-R1 leaves about 24 test prefixes per seed, which is why its standard deviations reach 0.10--0.17. Coverage alone does not distinguish a useful interval from a trivially wide one; we therefore report width and unbounded fraction throughout. Censored capacity/cost results are measured against an interpolated label. Finally, savings and Cost-of-Pass are counterfactual replays: they assume adding the guard would not change agent behaviour and that the sample is representative. The worked example in \S\ref{sec:econ} also shows what the guard does not do: its intervals are roughly twice as wide as the agent's, and the price of a calibrated answer is a less precise one. Where a deployer only needs a yes-or-no on whether the run still fits, that width costs nothing; where a tight point estimate matters, the interval is a bound, not a forecast. On the split behind that example, the gate aborts none of the 16 feasible test runs and recovers 4.9\% of doomed-run spend; Table~\ref{tab:abort} gives the five-seed means.
 
 \paragraph{Broader impact.}
 The intended effect is to reduce wasted compute, energy, and money on agent runs that cannot succeed; the Warehouse feasibility gate at $\delta{=}0.10$ illustrates that potential in one environment (86.3\% of doomed-run spend avoided in replay, without using remaining-budget intervals). The corresponding risk is that a stopping threshold set too aggressively terminates runs that would have finished; $\delta$ calibration exists to bound that risk at a user-chosen level, and the counterfactual caveat above means a deployer should not treat the reported savings as a guaranteed live return when choosing $\delta$, nor assume token environments will see Warehouse-scale savings.
